realloc returns the SAME block when the one it has is already big enough.

WHAT THIS IS ACTUALLY TESTING. Patch 0011's third round ends with a named
hypothesis and no way to choose between it and its alternative:

    "a pointer from section_ptr_add held across a later section_realloc would
     write through a stale base, which is the classic shape"

The reason that shape is worth suspecting HERE and not in tcc generally is an
asymmetry between this allocator and glibc's, and it is total:

    glibc realloc     grows IN PLACE whenever the chunk has room, which for a
                      doubling growth pattern is most of the time
    M2libc realloc    ALWAYS allocates a new block, copies, and frees the old
                      -- unconditionally, on every call, even a SHRINK

So a caller that holds a pointer across a realloc is correct-by-luck under
glibc and wrong on every single call under M2libc. That is exactly the
difference between the gcc-built control, which passes tcc's whole suite, and
mc-tcc, which corrupts its heap -- from the SAME SOURCE. Nothing else in the
comparison differs that way.

WHY THIS IS A FIX AND NOT A MASK. M2libc rounds every allocation up to a power
of two at or above 256:

    size_t used = 256;
    while(used < size) used = used << 1;

so a node's block is nearly always larger than what was asked for, and a
`realloc` that grows from 300 to 400 bytes is asking for room the block
ALREADY HAS. Returning it is what every production allocator does, it is
permitted by ISO C, and it is what the node's own `size` field is for. The
current code allocates 512 bytes, copies 300, and frees 512 -- for nothing.

It also costs: tcc's `tal_realloc_impl` and `cstr_realloc` both grow by
doubling, so every CString and every section in the compile currently pays a
full copy per growth, and every copy is a chance for the fault above to fire.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It does not shrink the block. Nothing here needs the
memory back and a shrink that reported a smaller size would then have to
handle a later grow across the old boundary, which is more bookkeeping than
this allocator has anywhere else. A shrink returns the same pointer with the
size unchanged, which is exactly what a rounding allocator does anyway.

READ THE NEXT RUN EITHER WAY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF LANDING IT:

  * if the corruption GOES AWAY, the fault was a pointer held across a
    realloc, 0011's hypothesis is confirmed, and the remaining work is to find
    and fix that caller in tcc rather than to keep the allocator quiet
  * if the corruption REMAINS, a stale realloc pointer is ruled out -- the
    class cannot fire any more -- and the node-loss detector's bracket is
    still there to name what does

Neither outcome is ambiguous, which is more than the current state offers.

--- a/stdlib.c
+++ b/stdlib.c
@@ -534,6 +534,37 @@ void* realloc(void* ptr, unsigned size)
 		return NULL;
 	}
 
+	/* IF THE BLOCK WE ALREADY HAVE IS BIG ENOUGH, KEEP IT.
+	 *
+	 * Every allocation here is rounded up to a power of two at or above
+	 * 256, so the block behind `ptr` is usually much larger than what was
+	 * asked for and a growth within that headroom needs no move at all.
+	 *
+	 * THIS HAS TO HAPPEN BEFORE malloc(), not after. Checking it later
+	 * would allocate the replacement block first and then leak it on
+	 * every in-place realloc, which on tcc's doubling growth is most of
+	 * them.
+	 *
+	 * Moving unconditionally is what made a caller holding a pointer
+	 * across a realloc fail on EVERY call under this allocator while the
+	 * same source is correct under glibc, which grows in place. See the
+	 * patch preamble.
+	 */
+	if(NULL != ptr)
+	{
+		struct _malloc_node* have = _allocated_list;
+		while(NULL != have)
+		{
+			if(have->block == ptr)
+			{
+				if(have->size >= size) return ptr;
+				break;
+			}
+
+			have = have->next;
+		}
+	}
+
 	void* new_alloc = malloc(size);
 
 	if(ptr == NULL || new_alloc == NULL)
